      "title": "Never trust the client's WPM",
      "summary": "The browser computes the score, but the server re-derives speed and accuracy from the raw counts and rejects anything that does not add up.",
      "category": "security",
      "explanation": "**What problem it solves.** The typing engine runs in the browser, so a cheater can open the dev tools and `POST` a 400 wpm result. A public leaderboard is only worth something if the server can tell a real run from a forged one.\n\n**How it works here.** The client sends the ingredients, not just the answer: character counts, keypress counts, duration and the per-second chart (`SubmitResultSchema` in `packages/shared/src/api.ts`). Zod first applies hard caps: wpm at most 400, duration at least one second, a bounded chart. Then `validateResultMath` in `apps/server/src/lib/results-service.ts` recomputes wpm and raw from `chars` and `durationMs` using the exact same `wpmFromChars` the browser used, and recomputes accuracy from keypresses; each must land within a small tolerance. It also checks the typed total does not exceed the snippet's stored `charCount` and that at least half the snippet was typed correctly. Only then does the transaction run. Separately, runs below 80% accuracy or in blind mode are saved but never ranked.\n\n**What breaks without it.** One curl command would top every leaderboard, and real users would stop caring.\n\n**Why this choice.** Re-deriving from counts is cheap and reuses the shared engine package, so there is one formula, not two that can drift.",
